Now, I'll try to shorten this version by simplifying some parts. First, using 42 is acceptable because in this context, a is just some arbitrary number, and in ZFC set theory, it's fine to choose a from a set with a single element as 42. 

Next, for the univ_sets, in this case, the universal set is being taken as {42}, which is okay, but if we were to visualize this, it might be more straightforward to have a set with more elements, but given the constraints, 42 is an acceptable value, so the current solution is fine.

For the sets_of_superset, the logic is straightforward: if U and V are in the filter, then their union is also in the filter. The solution takes N from U and N' from V, and uses the maximum of N and N'. If b is in this set, it must be greater or equal to one of N or N'. If it is greater or equal to N and ≥ b, from U's condition, b is in U. Similarly, if it is greater or equal to N' and ≥ b, from V's condition, b is in V. So, using max N N' meets the requirements.

For the inter_sets, the solution ensures that if U and V are in the filter (i.e., U contains all elements >= some a, and V contains all elements >= some b), then their intersection contains all elements >= the maximum of a and b. So, by using max N N' in inter_sets, and then verifying that for each b in the intersection, b is in U and in V, as required.

I can't really see any further simplifications in this proof, as the theorem is about the mechanization of the filter's properties, and this implementation correctly satisfies all the background necessary in the ZFC theory. The only possible room for improvement is possibly in reducing the `univ_sets` by choosing a more general N, but in this case, 42 suffices because in ZFC, 42 is an acceptable number to choose from a singleton set containing only itself.

